1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,321
The process map shows who does what, in which order, and where information is exchanged.

2
00:00:06,221 --> 00:00:07,526
First, the process.

3
00:00:07,526 --> 00:00:12,750
A site quality inspector identifies a defect, and a BIM coordinator logs it.

4
00:00:12,750 --> 00:00:18,660
The quality manager triages and assigns it to a trade subcontractor, who rectifies it.

5
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:23,540
The inspector verifies the work, and the defect is closed, or reopened.

6
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:29,175
Every day at five p.m., a defect status report goes to the owner's representative.

7
00:00:30,075 --> 00:00:30,795
Now draw it.

8
00:00:30,795 --> 00:00:36,433
The pools and lanes are already there: they were created from the actor roles of the use case.

9
00:00:36,433 --> 00:00:42,790
The general contractor has three lanes; the subcontractor and the owner's representative have a pool each.

10
00:00:43,690 --> 00:00:49,388
Select the start event in the inspector's lane, and append a task from its context pad.

11
00:00:49,388 --> 00:00:51,157
Name it 1, Identify defect.

12
00:00:52,057 --> 00:00:57,904
The inspector hands over a form, so add a data object from the palette, place it next to the task,

13
00:00:57,904 --> 00:00:59,993
and name it Defect inspection form.

14
00:01:00,893 --> 00:01:05,811
Then connect the task to the data object: the form is the output of the task.

15
00:01:05,811 --> 00:01:11,495
Data objects mark where information is exchanged; they will become exchange requirements.

16
00:01:12,395 --> 00:01:15,411
Drawing the whole process takes a while.

17
00:01:15,411 --> 00:01:23,553
Close the project without saving, and open the catch-up file DMBCF_5c_process_map.idm with the complete map.

18
00:01:24,453 --> 00:01:25,723
Here is the full map.

19
00:01:25,723 --> 00:01:32,922
Three pools: the general contractor with three lanes, the trade subcontractor, and the external owner's representative.

20
00:01:33,822 --> 00:01:40,903
Gateways make decisions: an invalid defect is rejected, and a failed verification reopens the defect.

21
00:01:40,903 --> 00:01:46,793
Message flows connect the pools, and a timer starts the daily reporting at five p.m.

22
00:01:47,693 --> 00:01:51,777
The eight data objects are the forms exchanged between activities.

23
00:01:51,777 --> 00:01:55,181
In the next step, each becomes an exchange requirement.

24
00:01:56,081 --> 00:02:03,142
Pools and lanes are linked to the actor roles of the use case: in Actor Roles, the tags Pool and Lane show the links.

